582
00:48:41,840 --> 00:48:47,951
So, if you're looking for, hey, this CVE is bad. We all agree.

583
00:48:47,951 --> 00:48:50,270
Don't run anything with a CVE.

584
00:48:50,270 --> 00:48:58,790
But what happens when it didn't have the CVE yesterday, and that's when it updated, and by the time we made the CVE, it was already million people affected. That's why EDR ultimately fails in this.

585
00:48:58,790 --> 00:49:10,830
The unfortunate sad part here is if you have credentials on your machine, and you are using any kind of build system right now, any kind of package manager, you're susceptible to this kind of attack.

586
00:49:10,830 --> 00:49:21,430
If you're using Open VSX, the extensions for VS Code, or all the things that use VS Code, that ecosystem, that is also being actively attacked right now where Make sure you're trusting your sources.

587
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:25,310
Make sure you're pulling from very reputable places.

588
00:49:25,310 --> 00:49:30,790
Pull them directly from vendors if and when possible, but as we just saw with Aqua, maybe that's not good enough anymore.

589
00:49:30,790 --> 00:49:34,590
That's where players like ChainGuard really stand out in my opinion, where they do the work.

590
00:49:34,590 --> 00:49:39,190
They do the work of rebuilding from scratch, and you're pulling their version, not the internet's version.

591
00:49:39,190 --> 00:49:45,111
They might pull the corrupted thing into their sandbox, but by the time you saw it, that malware is gone. I mentioned them by name.

592
00:49:45,111 --> 00:49:52,550
There's other people that do that out there, but that's the reality we're facing now is there's no good way to trust a binary anymore.

593
00:49:52,550 --> 00:49:54,670
There's no good way to say we trust you to never be compromised.

594
00:49:54,670 --> 00:49:58,230
And that's why these recent attacks really hit me as hard as they have.

595
00:49:58,230 --> 00:49:59,910
Like personally, this freaks me out a bit.

596
00:49:59,920 --> 00:50:01,710
I'm going to be complete open kimono.

597
00:50:01,720 --> 00:50:03,430
Like complete open chest here.

598
00:50:03,430 --> 00:50:16,110
I'm a little bothered by how fast this is all happening and specifically what we're seeing and what this means for the developer life where I used to just have environment variables on my local and that was how it worked and that's just that was safe.

599
00:50:16,110 --> 00:50:18,190
And you had to get a remote access into my machine.

600
00:50:18,190 --> 00:50:23,310
You had to look over my shoulder and hope that I exposed one of those.

601
00:50:23,310 --> 00:50:28,750
And now it is you trusted a security tool that was keeping you safe up until it didn't.

602
00:50:28,750 --> 00:50:31,710
And now everything in your machine is on the internet is public.

603
00:50:31,720 --> 00:50:36,350
I think that's a good place to end the show on Dwayne.

604
